[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: One node per file, because an agent reads files and traverses edges

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-ONE-NODE-PER-FILE
TITLE: One node per file, because an agent reads files and traverses edges
DEPTH: implemented
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
A file holds exactly one node. One requirement, one plan step, one plan, one
milestone, one backlog item, one decision -- one. The operator's word for it is
ONE.

The graph is a web pulled in by edges, not a set of documents read whole. That
only works if the unit a reader fetches is the unit the graph addresses. Before
the carve the addressable unit was a node and the fetchable unit was a file, and
the two were off by two orders of magnitude.

The corpus now satisfies this. DEC-NODE-FILE-NAMING carries how a file is named
and what a plan's backlog became, and MECH-SDOC-LAYOUT-CHECK carries the guard
that would keep it true, which does not exist yet.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
This was a context-economy defect before it was a tidiness one, and the system's
stated purpose makes it acute: DEC-SYSTEM-PURPOSE optimizes for a reader with a
bounded context and no memory of yesterday. A layout that forced that reader to
take 279 KB to learn 2.7 KB was working against the thing the graph is for.

It also compounded silently. Every session that filed a backlog item made the
next session's read more expensive, and nothing reported the cost.

The operator expects this to get clearer once semantics land and each item type
can have its own grammar, which is when a file's shape and its single node's
type become the same statement.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Raised by the operator 2026-08-27 after reading the file sizes: "thats a failure
mode ... 273k backlog loaded into context churning with re-reads ... you are
supposed to pull in from the graph what you need from edges."

The measurement that carried the argument, taken before the migration: 421 KB
across 156 nodes in 8 files, so an average node was about 2.7 KB. Reading one
node out of the backlog document cost 279 KB, because that file held 110 of
them. Reading one out of the executable-rules document cost 74 KB for 7 nodes,
10.5 KB apiece. An agent that opened the backlog to read one item took the
entire backlog into context, roughly a hundred times what it came for, and did
it again on every re-read.

Migration ran the same day, in ten commits from 667d0404 to ccad4c9d, and did
NOT wait for the writing tool from SLICE-SDOC-CLI as this node originally
wanted. The export is the validator, so a script splitting at node boundaries
plus a clean-directory export per step was sufficient. Node bodies were copied
byte for byte and only the document preamble was regenerated; the UID never
changes, so no edge moved. Proof rather than assertion: a per-node dump of the
JSON export, every field but the within-document _TOC position, is identical
before and after, and 0 of 161 contract hashes changed.

After: 161 nodes in 161 files, 447,724 bytes, 2,780 bytes on average. The
largest file is one node, slice-behaviour-model.sdoc at 17.9 KB, which is the
node's own size and not a packing problem.

DEPTH is implemented rather than verified: the carve was checked by the session
that ran it and by a contest over its findings, but no independent session has
read this node against the tree.

Adjacent but different, and now dissolved:
MECH-GROOMED-NODES-STAY-IN-THE-BACKLOG-FILE was about groomed nodes not moving
to a numbered document. That was a question of WHICH file; this was a question
of how many nodes a file may hold. Under this rule the other node's problem
went away, since a groomed node no longer moves at all.

Tool reads are a separate lever and were not in scope. The operator's words: "we
can tune later to not have as many tool reads, but for now thats a failure
mode."
<<<
